repoctl packaging
=================

The RPMs of repoctl and its RPM repo on GitHub Pages. The design is in
docs/design/tool.html, the phase in docs/design/delivery.html#phase-4.

Files

  repoctl.spec          the tool RPM, one per Fedora release; it packages
                        the binary that build.sh makes with the Go of go.mod
  repoctl-release.spec  the release RPM: the key file and the repo file
  repoctl-mirror.spec   the mirror job RPM (contrib/mirror), noarch
  repoctl.repo          /etc/yum.repos.d/repoctl.repo
  RPM-GPG-KEY-repoctl   the public tool repository key (not here yet: see
                        "Before the first release")
  releasevers           the Fedora and EL releases of the RPM repo
  yanked.txt            the bad releases (tool.html#tool-yank)
  build.sh              the binary and the unsigned RPMs, in containers
  sign-tree.sh          signs the RPMs, makes and checks the RPM repo
  import-key.sh         imports TOOL_REPO_KEY in the release and yank jobs
  test-install.sh       the first install in a container (CI)
  rpmtree/              the layout of the RPM repo: retention and yank list

Workflows (.github/workflows/)

  rpm.yml      pull requests: build, sign with a test key, install in a
               container of each release of releasevers
  release.yml  a tag v* on main: build; after a maintainer approves the
               job sign in the environment tool-release: sign, GitHub
               Release with attestations, then docs.yml
  yank.yml     manual, on main: the RPM repo without the yanked versions
  docs.yml     deploys the docs and the rpm-repo.tar of the newest release

Before the first release (once)

  1. Make the key on a host without network (tool.html#tool-key). Give it
     a passphrase:

       export GNUPGHOME=$(mktemp -d)
       gpg --quick-gen-key 'repoctl tool repository' rsa4096 sign 2y
       gpg --list-keys --with-colons | awk -F: '$1 == "fpr" { print $10; exit }'

     Keep one copy of the secret key offline: the export keeps the
     passphrase.

       gpg --armor --export-secret-keys FPR >repoctl-tool-key.offline.asc

     The environment secret needs the key without a passphrase. Remove the
     passphrase in a copy of GNUPGHOME only:

       cp -a "$GNUPGHOME" "$GNUPGHOME.ci"
       GNUPGHOME=$GNUPGHOME.ci gpg --passwd FPR        # empty passphrase
       GNUPGHOME=$GNUPGHOME.ci gpg --armor --export-secret-keys FPR >ci.asc

  2. In the repository settings, add the environment tool-release:
     required reviewers are the maintainers; deployment branches and tags
     are main and v*. Add the environment secret TOOL_REPO_KEY with the
     content of ci.asc. Then delete ci.asc and GNUPGHOME.ci (shred -u).
     No repository or organization secret holds the key.

  3. Commit on main, in one reviewed commit:
       - packaging/RPM-GPG-KEY-repoctl: gpg --armor --export FPR
       - internal/doctor/toolkey.go: ToolKeys = "FPR:current:YYYY-MM-DD"
         (the expiry date of the key)
       - the fingerprint in README.md and in the key table of
         docs/design/tool.html

  4. Tag the first release on main and push the tag:

       git tag -s v0.1.0 -m 'repoctl 0.1.0' && git push origin v0.1.0

     Approve the job sign of the workflow release in the Actions tab.

  5. Put the sha256 of repoctl-release-1-1.noarch.rpm (in the release notes)
     into README.md and into the row "First install" of the key table of
     docs/design/tool.html.

Each release

  Tag on main (git tag -s vX.Y.Z), push the tag, approve the job sign. A
  pre-release tag (vX.Y.Z-rc.N) makes a GitHub pre-release, which the
  Pages repo does not take.

  A change of RPM-GPG-KEY-repoctl or repoctl.repo raises Version in
  repoctl-release.spec, and changes the sha256 of the first install
  (step 5) after the release. A version of repoctl-release that the RPM
  repo already has keeps its released file.

  A new Fedora release: add it to releasevers. A Fedora release at end of
  life: remove it; the next release drops its directory.

Bad release, key rotation, key compromise

  tool.html#tool-yank, #tool-rotation and #tool-compromise. The yank job
  is the workflow yank on main.

Test the scripts on a host with docker (or CONTAINER=podman):

  export GNUPGHOME=$(mktemp -d)
  gpg --batch --passphrase '' --quick-gen-key 'test' rsa4096 sign 1y
  FPR=$(gpg --list-keys --with-colons | awk -F: '$1 == "fpr" { print $10; exit }')
  gpg --armor --export "$FPR" >/tmp/key.asc
  packaging/build.sh v0.0.1 /tmp/key.asc dist
  packaging/sign-tree.sh "$FPR" /tmp/key.asc dist "" out
